Now I see something interesting! Look at `mconvert` for `FILE_BEFLOAT`:

```c
case FILE_BEFLOAT:
    p->l = BE32(p);
    if (cvt_float(p, m) == -1)
        goto out;
    return 1;
```

This reads `p->l` (which is `uint32_t` at offset 0 of the union) and then calls `cvt_float(p, m)`. But `cvt_float` expects `p->f` (which is `float` at offset 0 of the union). Since `p->l` and `p->f` are at the same offset, this is fine.

But wait, `p->l = BE32(p)` reads 4 bytes from `p` (which is `p->hs` or `p->hl`). `BE32(p)` is a macro that reads 4 bytes from `p` in big-endian order. Let me check:

